# What each segment of engb is made of, as targets.
#
# A `base' line is what a phoneme's parameter does at a stress, whatever
# is either side of it. Any other line names a SET of phonemes that may
# stand to the left and a set that may stand to the right, `.' for a
# word edge, and says what those contexts do instead. A set is written
# as the phonemes run together.
#
# The numbers are the targets the parameter reaches, in order. One
# written `from:to' starts somewhere other than where the stretch before
# left off, which happens for two fifths of the aspiration's stretches
# and a seventh of the third formant's. A
# trailing `>' means the last stretch has no target of its own: the
# engine draws no breakpoint there and the stretch joins the next
# segment's first. What it
# starts from is whatever the segment before left behind and is not here;
# how long each stretch takes is the duration model's and is not either.
#
# The base of a parameter a context cannot move is in engb.phonemes,
# one line a phoneme; only the eight it can are based here. Every
# parameter's exceptions are here either way.
#
# Harvested by tools/module/segments.py. See docs/authoring.md.
